  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="wrap header-row">
      <div>
        <h1>Team Five Bits</h1>
        <p class="tagline">Five humans, one agent, zero deployed bugs (so far).</p>
        <nav aria-label="Page sections">
          <a href="#team">Team</a>
          <a href="#future-of-se">Future of SE</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
      <button id="theme-toggle" type="button" aria-label="Switch to night mode">
        <span class="theme-icon" aria-hidden="true">☾</span>
      </button>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="wrap">
    <section id="team">
      <h2>Who we are</h2>
      <div class="intro">
        <p>We are Five Bits, five M.Sc. Computer Science students at Paderborn University. We met in the Agentic Software Engineering lab, where we learned to build, question, and supervise AI coding agents. We share a taste for practical tools, honest opinions, and coffee-driven debugging. Our motto: ship small, review carefully, and never trust a confident autocomplete.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="member-grid">
        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:Photos/Dhruv.png}}" alt="Photo of Dhruv">
          <h3>Dhruv Savani</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester</p>
          <p>Dhruv studies computer science and is currently in the first semester of the M.Sc. programme. He is interested in backend development, cloud infrastructure, and open-source tools, and he enjoys automating repetitive infrastructure work. Dhruv sees AI assistants as a fast junior colleague: excellent for boilerplate, refactoring, and explaining unfamiliar code, but still capable of confident mistakes that need careful review. He believes the best results come from using AI to move faster without giving up understanding.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact">Fun fact: he cannot start a coding session without first reorganising his terminal setup.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI won't replace developers, but developers who ignore it will fall behind those who use it well.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:Photos/Harshil.png}}" alt="Photo of Harshil">
          <h3>Harshil Zadafiya</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester</p>
          <p>Harshil is a first-semester M.Sc. Computer Science student who enjoys cybersecurity, DevOps, and automation. He has a habit of automating small daily tasks even when doing them by hand would be faster. In his view, AI speeds up scripting, log analysis, and configuration work, but generated code can quietly introduce insecure patterns or outdated dependencies. He therefore treats security review and testing as essential.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact">Fun fact: he automates small daily tasks even when doing them by hand would be quicker.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 Every line of AI-generated code is a security review waiting to happen.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:Photos/Jevin.png}}" alt="Photo of Jevin">
          <h3>Jevin Vaghasiya</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester</p>
          <p>Jevin studies computer science in the first semester of the M.Sc. programme. His interests are machine learning, mobile app development, and competitive programming. He learns new programming concepts best by explaining them out loud to a rubber duck. Jevin finds AI useful for explaining algorithms and suggesting alternative solutions, but he believes beginners should try problems first so they do not skip the struggle that builds real problem-solving skills.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact">Fun fact: he learns new programming concepts best by explaining them out loud to a rubber duck.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 Code is getting cheap; knowing what to build and why is the skill that stays expensive.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:Photos/Pooja.png}}" alt="Photo of Pooja">
          <h3>Pooja Garg</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester</p>
          <p>Pooja is a first-semester M.Sc. Computer Science student interested in data science, user experience, and software testing. She reads error messages for fun and often finds them more informative than the documentation. Pooja uses AI for writing tests, summarising data, and exploring ideas quickly, but she validates its output against real data and tests because it can sound right while being wrong. Careful questioning and verification are the skills she values most.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact">Fun fact: she reads error messages for fun, and they usually tell her more than the documentation.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI writes the code fast; tests decide whether anyone should trust it.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:Photos/Yasik.png}}" alt="Photo of Yashik">
          <h3>Yashik Khunt</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester</p>
          <p>Yashik studies computer science and is currently in the first semester of the M.Sc. programme. He is interested in web development, UI design, and data visualisation. A weekend-long bug hunt once ended with a single missing semicolon, which he now treats as a cautionary tale. Yashik finds AI very good for generating UI components and quick prototypes, but he insists that design decisions, accessibility, and judgment still need a human who understands the users.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact">Fun fact: he once spent a whole weekend hunting a bug that turned out to be a single missing semicolon.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 Prompting is the new Stack Overflow: useful until you stop understanding the answer.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="stats">
      <h2>Team statistics (totally scientific)</h2>
      <div class="stats-grid">
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-number">5</span><span class="stat-label">humans</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-number">1</span><span class="stat-label">coding agent</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-number">47</span><span class="stat-label">cups of coffee per week</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-number">3.2</span><span class="stat-label">prompts until the website worked</span></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="future-of-se">
      <h2>The Future of Software Engineering</h2>
      <p>After working with coding agents in this lab, we agree on the following points:</p>
      <ul class="future-list">
        <li>AI will make the first draft of code cheap, shifting value towards problem framing, integration, and verification.</li>
        <li>Security review and testing will become more important, not less, because generated code can fail confidently.</li>
        <li>Teams will supervise agents with clear guardrails, checkpoints, and human ownership of every change.</li>
        <li>Human judgment about users, product decisions, and maintainability will remain central.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="disagreement">
        <p><strong>Where we disagree:</strong> some of us think AI will let small teams ship whole products that once needed large engineering organisations. Others think integration, reliability, and accountability will keep teams larger and more specialised. Both sides agree that the tooling will change faster than the processes around it.</p>
      </div>
      <h3>Our predictions</h3>
      <ul class="predictions">
        <li>2028: every pull request includes an agent-authored summary and a human-written risk note.</li>
        <li>2031: specifying intent and verifying behaviour is part of normal engineering, not a separate prompting skill.</li>
        <li>2035: code review focuses on system behaviour and security rather than typing style.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p>Built by an agent, supervised by humans. · 2026 · Team Five Bits</p>
    </div>
  </footer>

  <script>
    (function () {
      var root = document.documentElement;
      var toggle = document.getElementById('theme-toggle');
      var icon = toggle.querySelector('.theme-icon');

      function apply(theme) {
        root.setAttribute('data-theme', theme);
        var night = theme === 'dark';
        toggle.setAttribute('aria-label', night ? 'Switch to day mode' : 'Switch to night mode');
        icon.textContent = night ? '☀' : '☾';
      }

      apply(root.getAttribute('data-theme') || 'light');

      toggle.addEventListener('click', function () {
        var next = root.getAttribute('data-theme') === 'dark' ? 'light' : 'dark';
        localStorage.setItem('upb-theme', next);
        apply(next);
      });
    })();
  </script>
</body>
</html>

